      "patient_response": "No, no, I don't touch any of that stuff. I mean, I don't even drink, not even a beer after work. Some of the guys on the floor, they go out to the bar down the street, you know, the one with the neon sign that's always flickering? They go there after second shift and just get completely wasted. But I never go with them. How could I? If I go out and get drunk, or, I don't know, smoke something, my mind would be foggy. And I can't afford a foggy mind, not with what's going on. \n\nI have to be sharp. Like I said, they're always watching, always waiting for me to slip up so they can mess with the system and change my numbers. If I was hungover, or high, or whatever, I wouldn't be able to catch them. I wouldn't hear the whispers as clearly, or notice when the pallets are moved just that little bit to throw me off. I need all my senses working at a hundred percent. \n\nMy husband sometimes has a glass of whiskey on the weekends, and he'll ask if I want one to \"unwind.\" Unwind! Can you believe that? How am I supposed to unwind when I know the minute I go back to work on Monday, the sabotage starts all over again? Alcohol just makes you sloppy, and I refuse to be sloppy. I don't take any drugs, either. No pills, no weed, nothing. I just drink coffee—lots of coffee, actually, from the breakroom machine, even though it tastes like burnt dirt—just to stay alert. So, no, absolutely no drugs or alcohol. I need to keep my head completely clear to deal with this whole mess.",
